NAME
       sshd - the OpenSSH server, which accepts ssh logins

SYNOPSIS
       sshd [-t] [-T] [-D] [-f config_file]
       systemctl start|stop|restart|reload sshd

DESCRIPTION
       sshd listens for connections from ssh clients (port 22 by default),
       checks who is connecting (public keys, passwords) and starts a login
       shell for them. On RHEL 9 it runs as the service sshd.service; you do
       not start it by hand. Its settings are in /etc/ssh/sshd_config and in
       the files /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf, which the main file includes
       near its top. For every setting the FIRST value sshd reads wins, so a
       drop-in file whose name sorts early (00-mine.conf) beats a later one.

       After changing a setting, test the files with sshd -t and then run
       systemctl reload sshd (or restart). A file with a mistake stops sshd
       from starting again, so always test first, especially when you are
       logged in through ssh yourself.

OPTIONS
       -t     Test mode: read the configuration and the host keys, report
              errors and exit. No output and exit status 0 mean the files
              are fine.

       -T     Extended test mode: print every setting with the value that is
              in effect, one per line, names in lower case.

       -D     Do not detach (what the service uses).

       -f FILE
              Read FILE instead of /etc/ssh/sshd_config.

EXAMPLES
       Forbid root logins and check before reloading:
              echo 'PermitRootLogin no' > /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/00-lab.conf
              sshd -t && systemctl reload sshd

       Which value is in effect?
              sshd -T | grep -i permitrootlogin

FILES
       /etc/ssh/sshd_config, /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/*.conf
       /etc/ssh/ssh_host_*_key   the server's host keys
       ~/.ssh/authorized_keys    the public keys allowed to log in as a user

NOTES
       Settings are read again for every new connection, but changes reach
       the running service reliably only after reload or restart. SELinux
       allows sshd to listen only on ports labelled ssh_port_t (see
       semanage-port(8)); the firewall must allow the port too.

SEE ALSO
       sshd_config(5), ssh(1), ssh-keygen(1), semanage-port(8)
